--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/4 key facts kept) ---
I read in the evening paper that more than two and a half million were out of work, and the coal and cotton trades were in dispute. I also read that a general strike in South Wales had thrown 150,000 miners out of work, and hopes were expressed that a temporary arrangement would be made to permit them to return to their jobs.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/5 key facts kept) ---
1. Read in the Daily Herald that 150,000 miners in South Wales had struck work.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/3 key facts kept) ---
I read in The Times that the new year opens gloomily, with more than two and a half million out of work, and the coal and cotton trades in dispute; I read that 150,000 miners are on strike in South Wales; I hope that the differences between miners and operators will be ironed out tomorrow, so that the men may return to work.
   ☾ Ada (forgotten by morning): She dreamt that she was scrubbing an office, and found a sovereign under a desk.
   ☾ Arthur (forgotten by morning): He dreamt that he was being praised by Mr. Thomas for his neat and accurate balancing of the ledger.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas: He dreamt that he promoted Hale to be chief clerk in the office.  Mr. Thomas wakes, and does not wake Mr. Hale; sleeps again, and again dreams the same dream.

